# Findings gitleaks is right to raise and that cannot be removed.
#
# The weekly sweep in .github/workflows/security.yml scans the whole history —
# gitleaks-action passes --log-opts only for push and pull_request events, so a
# scheduled run has none and reads every commit. A finding in a commit that is
# already public therefore fails that job every Monday, whatever the working
# tree contains.
#
# These three are one invented identifier in triage evaluation data, shaped like
# an AWS temporary access key ID (ASIA followed by sixteen uppercase
# alphanumerics), which is enough for gitleaks and GitHub's secret scanning to
# match. Nothing was ever behind it. The placeholder was replaced in 124de21
# with a hyphenated form that cannot match, but 6aedfcf is pushed and public, so
# the history keeps it.
#
# Each entry is commit:file:rule:line. They pin one commit and one file, so a
# real credential committed anywhere else still fails the scan.
6aedfcff4dc49c75dee8fde9ca0209ce1ef62ed4:scripts/triage_eval_cases.json:aws-access-token:91
